  "text": "None of the three solutions can be confirmed, so I'm answering NONE. I could not verify the classification from memory, and each proposed answer depends on a step that is either unproven or doubtful.\n\n- **Solution 1 (12):** Its diamond case is the weakest. A diamond subdivision is an induced theta, so the diamond-subdivision-free class is the theta-free graphs. The proof claims every block of such a graph is a clique or a cycle. Layered wheels are theta-free and triangle-free but are not cycles or cliques, so that claim looks false. Whether the diamond still belongs in $\\mathcal M$ depends on the clique-width of layered wheels, which I could not settle, so this is a gap, not a disproof.\n- **Solution 2 (3):** It asserts that $\\mathcal M=\\{P_4\\}$ but never rules out the paw. That step is needed for the answer and is missing. The argument for $P_4$ itself, that its class is the cographs, is correct.\n- **Solution 3 (7):** It openly admits that the key step, bounded clique-width for pan-free (paw-subdivision-free) graphs, is unproven. Its handling of the diamond also rests on a claim about $K_{2,3}$ that it does not justify.\n\nThe question asks for a sum over several edge counts, which suggests $\\mathcal M$ has more than one member, so 3 is less likely than 7 or 12. That is a hint, not a proof.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
